1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,864
This is an easy step to under-rate, because it looks like the boring part after the

2
00:00:04,864 --> 00:00:05,789
interesting part.

3
00:00:05,789 --> 00:00:06,714
It is not boring.

4
00:00:06,714 --> 00:00:11,282
It is the exact place blast radius, from Section 1.3, becomes a real setting instead

5
00:00:11,282 --> 00:00:12,098
of a principle.

6
00:00:12,098 --> 00:00:13,948
Picture the workflow as it stands.

7
00:00:13,948 --> 00:00:18,408
An email arrives, the agent reads it, and a line of text, URGENT with a reason, or

8
00:00:18,408 --> 00:00:20,257
SKIP, lands on the wire and stops.

9
00:00:20,257 --> 00:00:22,269
Nothing downstream exists to hear it.

10
00:00:22,269 --> 00:00:26,784
Run it a hundred times and you have a hundred verdicts filed in a log nobody opens,

11
00:00:26,784 --> 00:00:28,959
which is why the slide calls it a diary.

12
00:00:28,959 --> 00:00:30,754
What turns a verdict into triage?

13
00:00:30,754 --> 00:00:35,269
Something has to fork on it: urgent goes one way, noise goes the other, and the two

14
00:00:35,269 --> 00:00:36,465
paths end differently.

15
00:00:36,465 --> 00:00:40,925
Routing is that fork, and it is also where you decide, in wiring, what the agent's

16
00:00:40,925 --> 00:00:42,394
words are allowed to cause.

17
00:00:42,394 --> 00:00:46,147
That is why this lecture belongs to blast radius and not to plumbing.

18
00:00:46,697 --> 00:00:51,381
A Switch node is a fork in the road, built entirely from a condition you write once.

19
00:00:51,381 --> 00:00:55,842
Here, the condition is almost embarrassingly simple: does the answer contain the

20
00:00:55,842 --> 00:00:56,511
word URGENT.

21
00:00:56,511 --> 00:01:01,140
It does not need to be clever, because the hard judgement already happened one node

22
00:01:01,140 --> 00:01:02,812
ago, inside the system prompt.

23
00:01:02,812 --> 00:01:05,489
The configuration is three choices in one panel.

24
00:01:05,489 --> 00:01:10,173
What to look at: the agent's answer, dragged in from the left the same way you built

25
00:01:10,173 --> 00:01:10,787
the prompt.

26
00:01:10,787 --> 00:01:15,081
How to compare: contains, not equals, and that one word is a small robustness

27
00:01:15,081 --> 00:01:15,471
lesson.

28
00:01:15,471 --> 00:01:20,155
Your agent writes URGENT and then a reason, so an equals check against the bare word

29
00:01:20,155 --> 00:01:23,612
would fail every single time while looking perfectly sensible.

30
00:01:23,612 --> 00:01:26,122
Contains matches the answer you actually get.

31
00:01:26,122 --> 00:01:30,137
And what to match: the word URGENT, in capitals, as the prompt demanded.

32
00:01:30,137 --> 00:01:34,486
Everything that does not match falls through to the other exit on its own, and

33
00:01:34,486 --> 00:01:39,171
notice what that quietly handles: the day the model gets creative and answers MAYBE,

34
00:01:39,171 --> 00:01:43,799
or apologises, or writes a paragraph, the strange answer drops safely into the skip

35
00:01:43,799 --> 00:01:45,695
path instead of crashing anything.

36
00:01:45,695 --> 00:01:50,045
The fallback exit is your first guardrail that costs literally nothing to own.

37
00:01:50,595 --> 00:01:53,089
Two details of this picture repay attention.

38
00:01:53,089 --> 00:01:57,794
The question in the diamond is deliberately dumb: one word, contains, done, because

39
00:01:57,794 --> 00:02:02,329
the intelligence already happened a box earlier and duplicating judgement in two

40
00:02:02,329 --> 00:02:04,993
places is how systems disagree with themselves.

41
00:02:04,993 --> 00:02:07,374
And the lower door is wider than it looks.

42
00:02:07,374 --> 00:02:12,079
It is not "answers that said SKIP", it is "everything that failed the urgent test",

43
00:02:12,079 --> 00:02:15,253
which is what makes it a safety net rather than a label.

44
00:02:15,253 --> 00:02:19,845
When you build your own forks, keep that shape: one smart box, one dumb question,

45
00:02:19,845 --> 00:02:24,210
and a default path that catches whatever the smart box does on a strange day.

46
00:02:24,760 --> 00:02:27,135
Step back and look at the whole shape now.

47
00:02:27,135 --> 00:02:29,623
Five boxes, one line of judgement, one fork.

48
00:02:29,623 --> 00:02:34,316
That is the entire agent, and it is also the exact shape from Section 2.3: trigger,

49
00:02:34,316 --> 00:02:35,956
node, connection, credential.

50
00:02:35,956 --> 00:02:39,123
Nothing about this build needed a fifth vocabulary word.

51
00:02:39,123 --> 00:02:40,876
Do the placeholders bother you?

52
00:02:40,876 --> 00:02:42,685
Good instinct, wrong conclusion.

53
00:02:42,685 --> 00:02:45,625
They are not scaffolding waiting for the real thing.

54
00:02:45,625 --> 00:02:50,375
They are what makes next week's trial measurable: every run now exits through a door

55
00:02:50,375 --> 00:02:55,125
with a name, so the execution log stops being a list of runs and becomes a tally you

56
00:02:55,125 --> 00:02:55,634
can read.

57
00:02:55,634 --> 00:02:59,649
How many emails left through the urgent door this week, and which ones?

58
00:02:59,649 --> 00:03:03,833
That is exactly the question Priya's one week trial has to answer, and the

59
00:03:03,833 --> 00:03:08,300
placeholders are what let her answer it by reading, not by re-running anything.

60
00:03:08,300 --> 00:03:12,485
When a real action does earn its place here, it will replace a named door,

61
00:03:12,485 --> 00:03:14,973
deliberately, with the job description open.

62
00:03:14,973 --> 00:03:17,121
That is how read only grows up safely.

63
00:03:17,671 --> 00:03:21,987
Do the renames now, while you still remember why each box exists, and before the

64
00:03:21,987 --> 00:03:25,332
next lecture's testing gives you something new to think about.

65
00:03:25,332 --> 00:03:29,810
Double click, type the sentence, move on: it costs less than a minute for the whole

66
00:03:29,810 --> 00:03:34,180
canvas, and it is the cheapest professionalism available anywhere in this course.

67
00:03:34,180 --> 00:03:38,604
Six months from now, this workflow will be something Dev opens cold in the Section

68
00:03:38,604 --> 00:03:42,812
10 rollout, and whether he can trust it will depend on whether it reads like a

69
00:03:42,812 --> 00:03:45,509
colleague's tidy desk or a stranger's junk drawer.

70
00:03:45,509 --> 00:03:49,717
The build is functionally complete: trigger, judgement, fork, two named doors.

71
00:03:49,717 --> 00:03:54,195
What is missing is proof that it actually works, without you spending a single real

72
00:03:54,195 --> 00:03:55,274
AI call to find out.

73
00:03:55,274 --> 00:03:56,407
That is next lecture.
